hanges._family_level (which keeps a level that rounds to 100 at its own digits). The brief gave words.py and build.py to V5. At merge, keep one template and one readable-level owner that both read, or a forest head and its Full results row can state one level two ways (L7).", "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "The cost chart's title and its axis each own the count the cost is per", "recommendation": "instance: campaign/aggregate.py:56 `_COST_PER = 1000` re-derives the ruler that blocks.py `_REPLIES_PER_COST_UNIT` (~1317) and ChartBlock._cost_drawn own. If one changes, the title states a different count than the axis it names. Take the words from the block's own ruler, or share the constant. WARNING at the boundary; batch with a planned commit.", "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Kit-side V4 behaviour is proven only through its static twin", "recommendation": "none: kLogTicks, kRowLines/kLabelText wrapping, kRangeBars' labels and head, scatter tags, the ink price line, kLevelHead and kTickFmt are all asserted on chart_svg output. A by-eye read finds the twins match (label rooms, margins, row pitch, tick choice, classes), and the plan names static-SVG proof for L8. No browser test catches a later divergence on the default (kit-drawn) page. The repo's convention accepts this. ACCEPT.", "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "The shared dots drawer keeps behaviour, with two intended shifts", "recommendation": "none: kDotLabelW/kNoteMargin/kRowLines/kDotRow and _dot_label_w/_note_margin/_row_lines/_dot_row give the old dot strip and barsH-dots geometry: the same label room (-12 vs -14-tagW), the same note cap (w*.3-10), the same hollow rule (barsH: lo==null; strip: r.hollow) and the same marks. _fit_label now reuses _two_lines. Two shifts: a dot strip whose labels wrap now takes 34 px rows (was 28, overlapping), and an explicit row_height smaller than a wrapped label's need is raised (barsH-dots used to keep it